  "query_preview": "..A strong cold front is currently sweeping through\nnorthern Utah, and will progress through the central and southern\nWasatch Front through 10 PM. Occasional gusts approaching 60 mph\nhave been noted in the wake of this front across the Salt Flats,\nand these winds will likely persist in the lee of the Raft Rivers\ninto the overnight hours owing to strong low level cold advection\nand strong pressure rises. As this front pushes into Utah County,\nanticipate downslope-prone areas in the northern portion of the\ncounty to see enhanced winds as well, and have issued a Wind\nAdvisory for this area into the overnight hours as well.\nAdditionally, webcams, satellite and surface obs all show\nconsiderable dust following the frontal boundary, which is\nreducing visibility to less than 3SM at times. With cooler air\nspreading into the region, daytime temperatures Thursday will\ntrend as much as 15 degrees cooler across northern Utah, and 5-10\ndegrees cooler across central and southern Utah. Despite this,\nthese temperatures will remain well above normal for mid May. \n \nBehind this system, zonal flow will develop across the area, with\nlargely dry, quiescent conditions expected Thursday and Friday.\nAnother storm system is likely to move through this weekend,\nlikely late Saturday into Sunday, which will bring cooler and\nwetter conditions once again. While there is still plenty of\nuncertainty regarding this system, models are generally in favor\nof a pretty stout cold front, with chances for showers and\nthunderstorms potentially both Saturday and Sunday, as well as\nfreezing temperatures for some valleys by Monday morning.",
